+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for helping improve this awesome list.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+Every submission must:
+
+1. Include a public `github.com` repository URL.
+2. Be relevant to lawyers, legal operations, contract review, legal research, document AI, privacy, OCR, RAG, or transcription.
+3. Be open source and inspectable from GitHub.
+4. Include the repository's stars, primary language, license, and last commit date.
+5. Fit an existing category or propose a new category with at least 5 credible repos.
+
+## Automatic Rejection Criteria
+
+We do not accept:
+
+- Proprietary SaaS products without a public GitHub repository
+- Dead or inaccessible repositories
+- Marketing pages instead of code repositories
+- Duplicate tools
+- Repositories with unclear legal usefulness
+
+## Recommended Pull Request Format
+
+Please include:
+
+- Repository name
+- GitHub URL
+- Why it is useful for lawyers
+- Stars
+- Primary language
+- License
+- Last commit date
+- Suggested category
+
+## Quality Standard
+
+Descriptions in the README should summarize what the repository actually says in its README or GitHub metadata. Avoid hype, unverifiable claims, or copied vendor marketing language.
+
+## Verification
+
+Before opening a pull request, confirm:
+
+- The GitHub link works
+- The repo is public
+- The repo license is visible on GitHub
+- The metadata is current
+- The tool is not a closed hosted service disguised as open source
